<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # First, I Brush My Teeth. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Evening Routine</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Brush My Teeth.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Express likes and dislikes. Interactive: Respond to simple instructions during activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can express preferences about bedtime (e.g., "I like bedtime stories."). Can express preferences about personal bedtime routines (e.g., " I like to brush my teeth before I go to bed."). Can follow and respond to instructions about bedtime (e.g., "Go to bed now." "Okay.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33.3%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33.3%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33.3%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, then displays flashcards of various evening routine actions (e.g., "brush teeth," "put on pajamas," "read a book," "go to bed"). The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and elicits known vocabulary from previous lessons. Students say the words chorally and individually. The teacher leads a quick "What's missing?" game with flashcards, encouraging students to orally name the missing action.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences about their own evening routine using sequencing words and present simple. "First, I wash my face. Then I brush my teeth. Next, I put on my pajamas. Last, I read a book and go to bed." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /b/ in "bed" and /t/ in "teeth" and blends /w-ɒ-ʃ/ in "wash" and /t-iː-θ/ in "teeth." Students repeat sentences chorally and individually, focusing on pronunciation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I brush my teeth in the morning or at night?" (Expected answer: "At night.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do I do *first*? Do I go to bed first, or brush my teeth first?" (Expected answer: "Brush your teeth first.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Evening Routine."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated short dialogue (with audio) between two children, Leo and Mia, discussing their evening routines. The dialogue uses sequencing words, present simple, and bedtime vocabulary. For example:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Leo: "Hi, Mia! What's your evening routine?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mia: "Hi, Leo! First, I wash my face. Then I brush my teeth. I like to brush my teeth! Next, I wear my pajamas. I feel sleepy. Last, my mom reads me a story. I like bedtime stories! Goodnight, Leo!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Leo: "Goodnight, Mia! Sleep well!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher plays the audio dialogue twice, pointing to the pictures. Students listen and follow along.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing evening routines: "First, I brush my teeth. Then I go to bed." Saying goodnight: "Goodnight, sleep well!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher asks simple oral gist questions.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Mia do *first*?" (Expected answer: "Wash her face.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does Mia like bedtime stories? Yes or No?" (Expected answer: "Yes.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Leo say to Mia at the end?" (Expected answer: "Goodnight, sleep well!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Sequencing words (First, then, next, last), Present simple for daily routines (I brush my teeth, I wear pajamas), Time and status expressions (It's bedtime, I am sleepy), Vocabulary (bed, pajamas, teeth, toothbrush, wash, wear, sleep, sleepy, tired, story).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Sentence Reordering:</b> The teacher provides students with cards showing jumbled parts of an evening routine sentence (e.g., "brush my teeth / I / Then"). In pairs, students orally arrange the words to form a correct sentence (e.g., "Then I brush my teeth.") and say it aloud. The teacher circulates, offering help and correcting pronunciation, especially /b/ and /t/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: Chain Drill:</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by saying, "First, I wash my face." The next student says, "Then, I brush my teeth." The following student says, "Next, I wear my pajamas." And so on, each student adding a step to the routine using a sequencing word and a routine action. The teacher encourages clear speech and corrects errors on the spot.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: "My Favorite Part":</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a set of picture cards representing different evening routine actions. Students take turns describing their routines to their partner, using sequencing words and expressing preferences. For example, "First, I wash my face. Then I brush my teeth. I like to brush my teeth! Next, I read a book. I like stories. Last, I go to bed." Partners listen and ask one simple follow-up question, like "Do you like to read books?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Evening Routine."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Pair Interview / Role-play – "Tell Me About Your Evening."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. One student (Student A) acts as an interviewer and the other (Student B) describes their evening routine. Student A starts by asking, "What is your evening routine?" Student B then describes their routine, using sequencing words (First, then, next, last), target vocabulary (wash, brush, wear, pajamas, teeth, book, story, bed), and expresses likes/dislikes (e.g., "I like to read stories."). They conclude by saying "Goodnight!" to each other. After 2-3 minutes, they switch roles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b> The teacher models the interview once with a student volunteer. The teacher circulates, listening for fluency and communicative success, encouraging students to use the learned expressions and politeness phrases ("Goodnight, sleep well!"). The teacher prompts students who are struggling with sentence frames like "First, I..." or "I like..." or "Then I..."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the pair interview (Stage 4). The teacher monitors student interactions, noting their use of target language and their ability to express preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their partner's evening routine to a small group or the class, summarizing what they learned. For example, "First, [Partner's Name] washes their face. Then they brush their teeth. [Partner's Name] likes to read a story." This requires listening and then speaking based on interaction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Oral Production):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /b/ in "bed," /t/ in "teeth," and blending in "wash," "teeth."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Includes 5+ vocabulary words from the theme (e.g., pajamas, toothbrush, story, sleepy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of sequencing words:</b> Uses "First, then, next, last" correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Expression of preferences:</b> Attempts to express likes/dislikes (e.g., "I like stories.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Responds appropriately to partner's questions and attempts goodbyes.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral):</b> The teacher asks students:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you use today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of your evening routine did you talk about?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite activity today? Why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult for you when talking about your evening routine?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? The chain drill and pair interviews generated good student interaction and use of sequencing words. Most students confidently used "First, I brush my teeth."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Some students still struggle with expressing "likes" and "dislikes" consistently, and incorporating polite phrases like "Can you read me a bedtime story, please?" needs more prompting. I will prepare more sentence frames for expressing preferences and asking for help for the next lesson.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking practice forms the foundation for Lesson 4, where students will learn to write simple sentences and paragraphs about their evening routines, building on the vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced orally today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">4 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>